BUILDER_DEFECT_FIXED=scripts/build_release_artifacts.py::clean_prior_artifacts() previously deleted by version-agnostic globs (*.whl, *.tar.gz, *-hermes-plugin.zip). Building directly into the repository dist/ therefore silently destroyed every previously released artifact; measured on a copy, dist/ dropped 12 -> 7 files and four hash-freeze gates went red. This defect predates 0.4.5 (earlier releases avoided it by building into a temp dir and hand-copying). Cleanup is now version-scoped to the current version's four formal filenames plus their -0.4.5- anchored .tmp leftovers. Two-way mutation: reverting to the wildcard globs -> 2 failed; skipping current-version leftovers -> 1 failed.

HISTORICAL_TEST_DEBT_CLEARED=51 pre-existing failures, all three root causes identified and fixed without weakening any security assertion. (1) 36 failures: an upstream coding executor had performed a global literal replacement inside scripts/run_r3c_wire_e2e.py, a file under AST freeze; restored byte-for-byte, canonical AST pin back to 5d97004c7a32d0cadbd44a6f163ce49d97a83f014bfccfdfd63a472290763c65. (2) 13 failures: sys.setprofile only instruments the calling thread while Hermes tool calls execute on a worker thread, so the probe silently missed them; observation layer moved to threading.setprofile. Production code untouched. (3) 2 failures: intermittent non-loopback audit contamination from api.github.com:443, root cause traced by stack probe to the Hermes host's hermes_cli/banner.py check_for_updates() daemon thread racing process exit; fixed by pre-seeding $HERMES_HOME/.update_check in the isolated environment so the host's own update ping hits its 6-hour cache. The network guard itself (tests/support/hermes_loopback_launcher.py) was NOT modified and no audit entry is filtered.

NETWORK_ISOLATION_PROOF=three-part. Repeat run 8/8 green; mutation removing the fix 6/6 red; assertion capability self-check confirms loopback accepted, api.github.com rejected, 10.0.0.5 rejected (the assertion was not loosened).

PHASE_FLAGS_TWO_WAY=tests/test_current_dist_policy.py CURRENT_DIST_PHASE=artifacts_landed and tests/test_reproducible_release.py ARTIFACTS_LANDED_FOR_CURRENT=True and the harness ARTIFACTS_LANDED=True must agree with one another. Both phases carry positive obligations: pending forbids the four artifacts and forbids any 64-hex for the 0.4.5 ZIP in README / designated report; landed requires the artifacts to exist and requires README, designated report, harness expectation, on-disk bytes and the versioned manifest to all publish the same hash. Neither phase permits a skip. Mutations verified red: deleting one artifact (6 failed), flipping one byte of the ZIP (3 failed), leaving stale pending wording in the report (1 failed), deleting a historical 0.4.4 artifact (3 failed), harness flag disagreeing with dist phase (1 failed), stale harness hash (1 failed), removing the resolve hash comparison (1 failed), README hash wrong (1 failed), README claiming pending (1 failed), README hash removed (1 failed).

OUT_OF_BOX_PROOF=Every C1/C6/C10 reading below was taken from the INSTALL TREE (0.4.5 ZIP extracted into a throwaway HOME/HERMES_HOME), never from the repository source tree. C1 fresh user with no store: Provider=1, not blocked. C1 counter-case, store present but corrupt: Provider=0, blocked. C6 redacted code recognised, plain text rejected, refusal payload carries CREDENTIAL_CODE_NOT_USABLE. C10 validate on a well-formed config: rc=0, "PASS credential tok" / "VALID"; on broken JSON: rc=1, FAIL CONFIG_INVALID_JSON; on an otherwise-valid config under an insecure (0755) parent directory: rc=1, FAIL CONFIG_PARENT_INSECURE_MODE. The last case is a deliberate counter-case proving validate is not merely permissive.

OUT_OF_BOX_MUTATION=Production-code mutations were applied INSIDE the ZIP and the entire hash chain (manifest + harness expectation) was realigned so that hash gates could not do the catching and the behavioural assertions had to carry the weight on their own. credential_guard/runtime_config.py::is_unconfigured_store_error forced to False (fresh users blocked, out-of-box regression) -> 2 failed. Same predicate forced to True (a corrupt store would be treated as benign, security regression) -> 2 failed. Both directions caught.

SOURCE_FALLBACK_MUTATION=Damaged install tree + healthy repo source + real Hermes PluginManager -> fail-closed with "No module named 'credential_guard'", loaded_from_source_tree=False, installed_from_zip=False, middleware_registered=False. Factor control deliberately places the repo root on preferred sys.path and the probe DOES detect source fallback, so the negative result is not vacuous.

ARTIFACT_COMPOSITION=39 ZIP members; every member byte-identical to its source-tree counterpart (identical=39, mismatch=0, not-in-source=0); zero test / script / doc / __pycache__ / dist-info contamination; zero credential-pattern hits (sk-*, PRIVATE KEY, ghp_*, AKIA*, synthetic decoys). ZIP sha256 equals the value recorded in dist/artifact-manifest-0.4.5.json.

C1_STORE_ROOT_HARDENING=Round-4 fix for a REGRESSION INTRODUCED BY C1 ITSELF. C1 (0.4.5) lets ordinary chat through when the secure store directory is absent, on the theory that absence means "never configured". That predicate could not distinguish a genuinely fresh profile from a MISDIRECTED HERMES_HOME (typo, deleted/renamed profile, stale inherited env, service unit with a wrong value), so an operator who HAD configured credentials silently lost redaction and sent plaintext. Measured five-scenario version matrix with one synthetic decoy, source tree swapped, everything else held constant: S4 (worker profile holds a real store, HERMES_HOME points at a non-existent dir) reads BLOCKED on 0.4.4, LEAKED on 0.4.5 pre-fix, BLOCKED on 0.4.5 post-fix. S2 (new profile, directory exists, no store) and S5 (fresh machine) stay PASS-THROUGH post-fix, so the out-of-box goal is preserved and was not traded away. Fix location credential_guard/config_lock.py::_assert_secure_store_dir: on FileNotFoundError the store ROOT (parent) is probed; root present -> CONFIG_LOCK_STORE_NOT_FOUND (C1 pass-through, unchanged), root absent -> CONFIG_LOCK_STORE_ROOT_NOT_FOUND (fail-closed). The probe reads ONLY the path HERMES_HOME itself resolves to and never another profile's directory, so multi-profile isolation is intact; a dedicated regression test asserts a sibling profile's credentials neither unblock nor leak into this profile's egress. Host context: hermes -p <name> already rejects a non-existent profile in hermes_cli/profiles.py::resolve_profile_env, so the reachable surface is a directly-exported or inherited HERMES_HOME, which hermes_constants.py consumes without an existence check. Mutations verified red: folding the new code back into the single legacy code -> import error (constant gone); root probe forced False (always pass through) -> 6 failed; root probe forced True (always block) -> 4 failed; removing the diagnostic-table entry -> 2 failed.

VACUOUS_REPLAY_ASSERTIONS_REMOVED=tests/test_r3c_wire_e2e.py::_assert_replay carried four checks of the shape ``r["second_*_delta"] in (0, None)``. Measured on a live run_all: all four are unconditionally None in all three replay scenarios, because the carrier only assigns them inside ``if len(tool_request_identities) >= 2`` and that profiler-derived list is empty under the worker-thread model. The checks therefore could not fail and pinned nothing. Replaced by whole-run production totals (resolve==1, adapter==1, target hits==1), which come from real counters rather than sys.setprofile, plus the pre-existing exact REFERENCE_PATH_BLOCKED code assertion. Readings are taken from the live run and perturbed, never written as literals, because the R3C false-green gate rejects hard-coded counter values in carrier files. Mutation: collapsing the new resolve assertion to a tautology -> 1 failed. A regex guard fails the suite if any ``in (0, None)`` replay assertion is reintroduced.

R6_MATRIX_REPOINTED_TO_CURRENT_RELEASE=scripts/run_r6_installed_zip_e2e.py pinned credential-guard-0.4.0-hermes-plugin.zip, which has never existed in dist/ on any commit (git ls-tree HEAD dist/ starts at 0.4.2). Every opt-in run therefore died with FileNotFoundError before installing anything: rc=1, all 15 matrix cells errored. Meanwhile three RETIRED tests in tests/test_r3c_wire_e2e.py cited this matrix as their "live equivalent coverage", and tests/test_r5_wire_e2e.py::test_r5_wire_full_main_chain_matrix_closed asserted only that files, function names and source substrings exist -- so the claim "all 15 green" had nothing behind it. This was an evidence-chain break, not merely a loose assertion. Repointed to the current release ZIP and the same thread-model / C8 / C9 adaptations already applied elsewhere were applied here: timeout-vs-deny now asserts the host's own untruncated approval record instead of the profiler-derived approval_outcome; replay now asserts whole-run totals plus the exact REFERENCE_PATH_BLOCKED code; the result-echo check accepts the C9 unified <SECRET:cg_[0-9a-f]{16}> marker while keeping the plaintext-absence assertion untouched. Opt-in suite now rc=0, 37 passed (was rc=1 with 15 cells erroring). Mutation, applied INSIDE the ZIP with the whole hash chain realigned so hash gates could not do the catching: disabling BOTH reference-path replay guards (tool_execution.py lines 197 and 519) -> 4 failed, 30 errors. Note the single-site variant is NOT sufficient -- disabling only line 519 leaves the line 197 guard closing the replay and the suite stays green, which is why both sites must be mutated together to demonstrate load-bearing.

C1_STORE_ROOT_NOT_A_PROFILE=Round-5 fix for the HALF the round-4 fix left open. Round 4 split C1 pass-through on "does the store ROOT exist". An independent read-only review then measured the other half: when HERMES_HOME names a directory that EXISTS but is not a Hermes home at all (stale value after a profile rename, a mount landing before provisioning, an inherited env var, an operator exporting a scratch path), the root-exists branch fired, C1 declared "never configured", and a configured operator's credential reached the wire in plaintext. 0.4.4 was fail-closed there, so this was a genuine regression of the same class as round 4, merely the other half of it. Measured with one synthetic decoy, everything else held constant: HERMES_HOME -> absent dir gives provider_calls=0 leaked=false; HERMES_HOME -> existing empty dir gave provider_calls=1 leaked=TRUE before the fix; HERMES_HOME -> /tmp gave provider_calls=1 leaked=TRUE before the fix; both now read provider_calls=0 leaked=false. The predicate is now four-way and fires ONLY when HERMES_HOME itself names the root under test (explicit path-identity comparison), so callers passing unrelated store directories -- tests, migrations, explicit --config paths -- are judged exactly as before: root absent -> ROOT_NOT_FOUND (fail-closed); root carries a Hermes marker -> STORE_NOT_FOUND (C1 pass-through); root unmarked AND is the explicitly exported HERMES_HOME -> ROOT_NOT_A_PROFILE (fail-closed); HERMES_HOME unset so the default ~/.hermes applies -> STORE_NOT_FOUND (C1 pass-through). That last row is what preserves the out-of-box goal: a genuinely new user never exports HERMES_HOME, they run hermes and land on the default home. Marker detection is any-of over HERMES_HOME_MARKERS (config.yaml, SOUL.md, MEMORY.md, plugins, skills, profiles, agent_sessions.db) and deliberately resolves OSError to "yes", so a future host layout change degrades into round-4 behaviour rather than blocking real users; the asymmetry is intentional because a false "yes" only restores the previous state while a false "no" would silently stop redaction. MULTI-PROFILE ISOLATION UNCHANGED: only the single path HERMES_HOME resolves to is ever inspected and no sibling profile is consulted, pinned by a path-level os.lstat/Path.exists trace assertion in tests/test_c1_store_root_not_a_profile.py. MUTATION, all four red in both directions: marker probe forced true (= revert to round-4 behaviour, S6 leaks again) -> 4 failed; marker probe forced false (= real new profiles blocked) -> 27 failed; explicit-root resolver forced None (= whole rule dead) -> 4 failed; path-identity comparison removed (= unrelated store dirs misjudged) -> 2 failed.

C1_ROUND5_FIXTURE_REALISM=Three round-4 fixtures and the isolated_home fixture in tests/test_c1c2_zero_config_usability.py created their profile root with a bare mkdir, a shape the host never produces -- hermes_cli/profiles.py::create_profile always scaffolds skills/, .env and SOUL.md. Those fixtures were modelling an unreachable state, and under the round-5 predicate they correctly fail closed. A scaffold marker was added so each fixture models a REAL profile whose store simply does not exist yet, which is what every assertion in them is actually about. NO ASSERTION WAS WEAKENED: this was verified by first adding the marker in a throwaway copy of the repository and observing every previously-failing case pass with zero assertion edits, then applying the same change to the working tree.

C1_ROUND5_TEST_POLLUTION_FIX=The new module's egress probe must evict credential_guard from sys.modules to re-import the package under a probe-controlled environment. Leaving that eviction in place handed every later test file a second, distinct copy of the package whose exception classes failed isinstance against the ones those files imported at collection time, producing 280 spurious failures across roughly 30 files on the first full run. The probe now snapshots sys.modules and restores it in a finally block. Full suite after the fix: 2302 passed, 3 xfailed, 0 failed, rc=0.

